# The categorization gold set (test plan section 12.3), the corpus behind acceptance criterion G2.
#
# FORMAT.  `LABEL|complaint text`, one item a line. Blank lines and lines starting with `#` are
# ignored. Pipe-separated rather than CSV because complaint text is full of commas and nothing
# here needs a quoting rule — see GoldSet, which parses it in one `split`.
#
# LABELS.  The four domains of section 1.5, plus `AMBIGUOUS` for an item a human reading it could
# not confidently place either. Section 12.3: an `AMBIGUOUS` item is expected to land in triage,
# and auto-accepting one is counted as an error, not as coverage earned.
#
# SPLIT.  There is no split column. Development and holdout are derived from the text itself
# (GoldSet.Item#isDevelopment), so which items the holdout holds was never anyone's choice —
# which is the whole point of a holdout. Section 12.3: tuning against it would produce a number
# that means nothing.
#
# PROVENANCE.  Every item is synthetic, hand-authored from the kinds of complaint Cauvery Hostel
# receives — not transcribed from the real response sheet. No resident's name, roll number, phone
# number, email or actual words appear here, and none ever will: a committed fixture is
# effectively permanent, so the scrub cannot be a later step (sections 11.4, 12.2).
#
# The wording is deliberately as bad as the real thing: misspellings, Tamil/English
# transliteration, SMS shorthand, and one-line messages with no verb. A corpus of well-formed
# English sentences would measure a classifier we are not shipping.

